      "worked": "For $x\\ne0,1$, multiply by $x(x-1)^2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}2x^2+1&=A(x-1)^2+Bx(x-1)+Cx.\\end{align*}$$\nThe resulting polynomial identity holds for every $x$, so substitutions at $0$ and $1$ are valid in this identity.\n\nLet $x=0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}1&=A.\\end{align*}$$\nLet $x=1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}3&=C.\\end{align*}$$\nEquate the coefficients of $x^2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}A+B&=2\\\\1+B&=2\\\\B&=1.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $A=1$, $B=1$ and $C=3$. The original rational identity holds for $x\\ne0,1$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
